_:r9932725f33a24a7f2ee43414_n30d24899417a4cd58033d35bd6dd48acb11 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#predicate> <https://concepts.datalad.org/s/things/v2/description_html> .
_:r9932725f33a24a7f2ee43414_n30d24899417a4cd58033d35bd6dd48acb11 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://concepts.datalad.org/s/things/v2/AttributeSpecification> .
_:r9932725f33a24a7f2ee43414_n30d24899417a4cd58033d35bd6dd48acb11 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#value> "<p> <a href=\"https://github.com/mykrok/mykrok\">MyKrok</a> (Ukrainian: \"мій крок\" — \"my step\") is a CLI tool to incrementally back up fitness activities from <a href=\"https://www.strava.com\">Strava</a>, including GPS tracks, photos, comments, and kudos. Backups are browsable offline through a built-in web frontend with interactive maps and activity statistics — applying the same git-annex / open-data philosophy of CON's archival tools to personal fitness data. Try the <a href=\"https://mykrok.github.io/mykrok/\">live demo</a> with synthetic data. </p>" .
_:r9932725f33a24a7f2ee43414_n30d24899417a4cd58033d35bd6dd48acb12 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://concepts.datalad.org/s/things/v2/Start> .
